===Etymology 3===
From earlier {{m|lv|*asus}}, from {{inh|lv|bat-pro|*ašus}}, from {{der|lv|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-||sharp, pointed, edgy}}. With an extra suffix ''*-ro'', the same stem also yielded {{cog|ine-bsl-pro|*aśras||sharp}}, whence Latvian dialectal {{m|lv|asrs}}, as well as the {{cog|lt|-}} cognate {{m|lt|aštrùs}}, dialectal {{m|lt|ašrùs}}. From this stem, there are also Latvian reflexes with ''ak'' rather than ''as'' (e.g., {{m|lv|akmens||stone}}, {{m|lv|akots||awn}}), possibly a result of Proto-Indo-European dialectal variation. In Latvian, former ''u''-stem adjectives like {{m|lv|*asus}} were assimilated into other classes; {{m|lv|*asus}} gave rise to both an ''o''-stem and a ''yo-''stem variant which later on became independent words, {{m|lv|ass}} and {{m|lv|ašs}}, with different semantic nuances (compare also, e.g., {{m|lv|plats}} and {{m|lv|plašs}}, or {{m|lv|dobs}} and {{m|lv|dobjš}}). Other cognates include {{cog|cu|остръ|sc=Cyrs}}, {{cog|ru|о́стрый}}, {{cog|be|во́стры|sc=Cyrl}}, {{cog|uk|о́стрий|sc=Cyrl}}, {{m|uk|го́стрий|sc=Cyrl}}, {{cog|bg|о́стър|sc=Cyrl}}, {{cog|cs|ostrý}}, {{cog|pl|ostry}}, {{cog|gem-pro|*agjō}} ({{cog|goh|ecka}}, {{m|goh|egga||corner, edge, point, peak, blade}}, {{cog|de|Ecke}}, {{cog|sa|अश्रि|अश्रिः|corner, edge, blade|tr=áśriḥ|sc=Deva}}, {{cog|grc|ἀκή||point, tip}}, {{m|grc|ἄκρος||sharp, pointed}}, {{cog|la|ācer||sharp}}, {{m|la|aciēs||sharpness, blade}}.<ref name="LEV"/>

